## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Observed by the coordinator on 2026-08-31 while gating the B073 merge.
Filed rather than dismissed, because the whole point of the counting gate is
that its verdict is trustworthy, and this one was not reproducible.

## What happened

Merged B073 to master, ran `just test`. It exited 1 and printed the
INCOMPLETE-RUN explanation ("... at the first failure, so everything after it
never runs either. Both shrink the total without producing a single failed
test. Do not report the number above. See B078.").

An IMMEDIATE re-run of the **identical tree**, no rebuild in between, printed:

    ==> 26/26 binaries reported, 27 sections
    SUITE-VERIFIED: 2397 passed; 0 failed; 14 ignored; 26/26 binaries reported

A third run, throttled with `-- --test-threads=2`, gave the same clean 2397.
So master at that commit is good, verified twice. **What is not established is
why the first run refused.**

## Conditions — likely relevant, NOT proven causal

Machine load average was **32-41 on a 16-core box** at the time, from four
concurrent lanes plus the gate.

The cause is a thread-count trap worth writing down: `cargo test -j2` caps
cargo's BUILD jobs but does NOT cap the test harness, which defaults to one
thread per CPU (16 here). `just test` does not set `--test-threads` — it is
opt-in via `just test -- --test-threads=2`. So five concurrent suites were up
to 80 test threads, not the 10 the `-j2` convention implies.

**No OOM occurred.** An initial `grep -i oom` over the journal appeared to
match, but it was matching "r-oom-s" in an unrelated service's log line. There
were no kills.

## Why this matters beyond one flake

**An INCOMPLETE RUN verdict is currently indistinguishable between:**

1. a test binary crashed from a real defect — the case the gate exists for;
2. a binary was starved, killed or timed out under machine load — environmental,
   and the gate says nothing about it;
3. the `--no-run` enumeration and the run disagreed for some other reason.

The gate did the right thing by refusing to print a total. The problem is that
its refusal is **not diagnostic**, so a human must guess — and the failure mode
of guessing is dismissing a real crash as "just load". That is very close to how
B078 nearly went, and it is the same shape as this batch's standing hazard that
a green result is not evidence until you know what made it green. An
unexplained RED is the same hazard wearing the other colour.

## Work

Routed to **B085**, which already owns making the gate see more than
binary-level liveness. When a binary does not report, capture and print what can
be known about WHY: process exit status or signal, whether it produced partial
output, and the machine load at the time. A SIGKILL under load average 40 and a
SIGSEGV on an idle box are different findings and must not render as the same
line.

**Do NOT fix this by retrying until green.** A gate that retries converts an
intermittent into silence, which is strictly worse than the current behaviour.

Also consider whether `just test` should default to a bounded `--test-threads`
rather than one-per-CPU. The repo's own etiquette rule is 2 threads for builds
and tests; the recipe currently honours that only for builds, and only if the
caller remembers.

## If it recurs

Capture the FULL output to a file, not a tail — the first occurrence's detail
was lost to `| tail -6` and that is why this ticket cannot name the binary. The
verdict line names the failing binary; the tail did not include it.

## Related

B082 (the counting gate this is about), B085 (where the fix belongs), B078 (a
flaky crash that was correctly NOT dismissed), B032 (a real env-var race in this
repo that high thread counts made far more likely).